      'RICHES MAKE THEMSELVES WINGS — the lesson of every market you have lived through, and the treasure that outlasts them all. Darrell forwarded The Daily Upside of 2026-11-06 into the app with a single word typed above it, Lesson. Many who read this band have watched more than one market fall, and some have watched savings meant for retirement shrink within a season. The Word speaks to that, plainly and kindly. THE REPORT, AND ITS LIMITS. The newsletter reported that on Thursday, 2026-11-05, two days after the national elections, technology shares fell sharply. By its account the Nasdaq Composite dropped 5.5 percent into correction territory, roughly $2.5 trillion of market value disappeared in one session, about $1 trillion of it among the seven largest technology companies, and Nvidia closed down 9.2 percent. It attributed the decline to investors anticipating that newly elected officials would slow the construction of data centers for artificial intelligence. It reported a volatility index up nearly 70 percent, a consulting firm’s tally of 44 local measures restricting data centers against one in favor, and a Pew finding that more than half of Americans regard data centers as harmful to the environment. We did not verify these figures against market data. One strategist suggested that some leveraged funds might be failing, which is a suspicion rather than a finding, and he and other analysts disagreed about what comes next. We make no forecast here, and nothing in this lesson is financial advice. AN OLD PROVERB FOR AN OLD PATTERN. "Labour not to be rich: cease from thine own wisdom. Wilt thou set thine eyes upon that which is not? for riches certainly make themselves wings; they fly away as an eagle toward heaven." (Proverbs 23:4-5) Solomon calls riches that which is not, and those who have lived long understand why. A portfolio statement is a photograph of prices on one particular day, not a sum locked in a vault. Its value rests on what millions of people expect, and expectations can turn between breakfast and lunch. The same newsletter, in another story, reported that a meal-kit company once valued near $1.9 billion was purchased for $103 million six years later, and that a delivery service bought for $7.3 billion in 2020 was valued at $650 million in 2024. "For riches are not for ever: and doth the crown endure to every generation?" (Proverbs 27:24) THE BARNS AND THE SOUL. Jesus’ parable in Luke 12 has comforted and corrected many believers in their later years. The rich man resolved, "And he said, This will I do: I will pull down my barns, and build greater; and there will I bestow all my fruits and my goods." (Luke 12:18) and then he instructed his soul to take its ease. Then Yahweh spoke: "But God said unto him, Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?" (Luke 12:20) Notice the question at the end of that verse, because every saver must eventually answer it. The fault was never the barn; Joseph filled granaries at Yahweh’s direction and preserved nations alive. The fault was a man who planned for decades and never once consulted Yahweh about any of it. "So is he that layeth up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God." (Luke 12:21) THE HUMBLE PLAN. "For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that." (James 4:15) Prudence is not fear. "A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished." (Proverbs 22:3) Those approaching or living in retirement understand the difference between money they may need next year and money they will not touch for many years, and they know that borrowed money can force a sale at the worst possible hour. Solomon put it in one line: "the borrower is servant to the lender" (Proverbs 22:7). A wise elder is often the one in the family who remembers that. WHAT ENDURES. "Charge them that are rich in this world, that they be not highminded, nor trust in uncertain riches, but in the living God, who giveth us richly all things to enjoy; That they do good, that they be rich in good works, ready to distribute, willing to communicate; Laying up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold on eternal life." (1 Timothy 6:17-19) Paul does not instruct the rich to despise what they possess; he tells them where to rest and how to use it generously. Job, who lost nearly everything in a single day, said, "And said, Naked came I out of my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return thither: the LORD gave, and the LORD hath taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD." (Job 1:21) And Jesus said, "But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal:" (Matthew 6:20) The years teach what the young must take on faith: the gifts given, the children taught, the neighbor helped, these do not fall with the market. THE PRICE NO MARKET SETS. A strategist described Thursday with an old trading-floor saying about blood. The Word reserves that word for a single purpose. "Forasmuch as ye know that ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold, from your vain conversation received by tradition from your fathers; But with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot:" (1 Peter 1:18-19) Jesus is the Lamb of Yahweh and the Eternal Son of Yahweh, and John the Baptist pointed to Him with these words: "Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world" (John 1:29). What He purchased is the one inheritance that can never be marked down. AN INHERITANCE THAT LASTS. "A good man leaveth an inheritance to his children’s children" (Proverbs 13:22). That inheritance is more than money. A grandchild who has watched you stay steady through a falling market, give when giving was hard, and refuse to borrow against your peace has received something no statement can show and no selloff can take. A WORD FOR THE ELDER. If you lost value this week, you are not foolish for feeling it. Grieve honestly, and do not sell your peace to the headline. Write down what you actually have and what you actually owe. Speak with someone qualified who can see your real situation before you make any decision. Then turn to the younger ones near you, and tell them what you learned from the falls you have lived through. That story may be worth more to them than anything you leave in an account. "Let your conversation be without covetousness; and be content with such things as ye have: for he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee." (Hebrews 13:5) TALK ABOUT IT TOGETHER. Grandparents, ask your grandchildren what they think money is for, and listen before you answer. Children and grandchildren, ask your grandparents to tell you about a hard money year they lived through and what they learned. With a friend your own age, share one way you have learned to trust Yahweh more than a balance, and pray for each other. Do it once today, at the table, on the way, at bedtime, or first thing. So we all get healthy together, until we see that Yahweh has been right.',
